    8. 
        👦 Nischal continues by asking the team,
        “Can you see the pattern?”
        “Candidates with a college degree have a higher promotion percentage than candidates
        without a college degree. We fed this 5-year data to our AI system, which selected only
        2% of candidates without a college degree for promotion in 2022. The AI system made its
        selection based on the company’s past promotion record.” 
    
    9. 
    Finally, the team could connect the concept with their promotion application.
        🧑‍🦰 Jordan joins the conversation and says: 
        “So, in our promotion application,
        -> Employees who have a college degree are in the privileged group.
        -> Employees who do not have a college degree are in the unprivileged group.
        -> Education, when used to separate the population into groups, introduces disparity, and
            is the protected attribute.”
    
    10.
        👦 Nischal confirms Jordan’s understanding and concludes the initial analysis, saying,
        “There are many ways through which bias can enter the AI system. We need to investigate
        more on how bias entered the promotion system. The type of fairness (group, individual,
        or both) to focus on depends on the problem. Many factors can influence the selection of
        the protected attributes, such as the organization’s principles, the application’s focus,
        and regulations. The team must consider all of the factors and carefully choose protected
        attributes. The Data Science team needs to design a way to check for bias throughout the AI
        lifecycle and take necessary mitigation action. Addressing fairness issues is a team sport!”